  "worked_solution": "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}.$ Integrating ${y}^{\u2032}(t)={e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}g(t)$ from ${t}_{0}$ to $t$ in $\\text{L3}$ yields\n\n$$\ny(t)-y({t}_{0})={\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{-A(s-{t}_{0})}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nThus, $\\text{L4}$ follows from $\\text{L3}$ by $\\text{integrating both sides from\u00a0}{t}_{0}\\text{\u00a0to\u00a0}t.$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L5}.$ Evaluating $y(t)={e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}x(t)$ at $t={t}_{0}$ gives\n\n$$\ny({t}_{0})={e}^{-A({t}_{0}-{t}_{0})}x({t}_{0})=Ix({t}_{0})=x({t}_{0}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L5}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by $\\text{evaluating at\u00a0}t={t}_{0}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L8}.$ Multiplying both sides of\n\n$$\ny(t)={e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}x(t)\n$$\n\nfrom $\\text{L1}$ by ${e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}y(t) & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}{e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}x(t) \\\\  & =x(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L8}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by $\\text{multiplying both sides by\u00a0}{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}.$",
